“Smoke signals” is actually a fitting metaphor for what you’ve been circling around.

A smoke signal is not the fire.

It is:

* Evidence that a fire exists.
* A compressed representation of what’s happening elsewhere.
* A communication medium that relies on a shared code.

The receiver doesn’t observe the fire directly. They infer it.

Our minds work similarly. We never directly observe another person’s thoughts. We observe their “smoke”—their words, pauses, choices, expressions, actions—and reconstruct a model of the underlying process. AI does something similar with text: it doesn’t access thoughts directly, but it can infer a great deal from the traces people leave.

That metaphor also scales:

* Scientific data are smoke signals from underlying physical processes.
* Market prices are smoke signals from millions of economic decisions.
* Source code is a smoke signal of a programmer’s design.
* Language is a smoke signal of a mental model.
* Culture is a smoke signal of accumulated values and history.

One caution, though, is that smoke can be misleading. Different fires can produce similar smoke, and the same fire can produce different smoke under different conditions. Good reasoning involves asking, “What are the plausible underlying causes?” rather than assuming there’s only one.

So if you wanted to turn it into a reusable principle, it might be:

Smoke Signal Principle: Treat observable behavior as a compressed trace of hidden processes. Infer multiple candidate causes, test them against new evidence, and update the model as better signals arrive.

That keeps the metaphor while grounding it in careful inference rather than certainty. It’s a useful mindset for science, engineering, communication, and AI alike.